// FunctionNameIndex — the name -> function index, and the ONE statement of the invariant it rests on.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE POLICY, IN ONE PLACE: AN INDEX OVER A MODULE'S FUNCTIONS HAS NO TIE-BREAK, BECAUSE IT REFUSES
// A TIE.** Several passes need to resolve a function NAME to the function it names, and every one of them
// rests on the same fact — a merged module holds no two functions of one name. Before A1w each carried its
// own copy of the index and its own answer to "what if it does": last-wins at three of them, FIRST-wins at
// the fourth, and a check at exactly one. That is one fact with four readers, and the reader that had
// learned it (A1t) was the only one that could say so. So the tie-break is gone rather than made uniform:
// a duplicate is DETECTED where the index is built — the collision was never something to predict, only
// something to notice (A1t) — and the compile is refused. Nothing downstream needs a policy for a state
// that cannot exist.
//
// ⭐ **WHY DETECTION IS FREE, AND WHY IT IS NOT A `contains` PROBE.** The loop inserts index `i` on
// iteration `i`, so the map's height IS `i + 1` after every insert unless one insert collapsed two names
// into one entry. That is one integer compare per function and no second hash probe. A `contains` ahead of
// each insert would answer the same question AND hand back the incumbent's index for free — but it doubles
// the hash probes on a per-function loop to save a back-scan that runs at most once, on a path whose next
// act is to refuse the compile.
//
// ⭐ **WHAT EACH CALLER CAN ACTUALLY SEE IS DIFFERENT, AND THAT IS WHY THE CHECK BELONGS HERE RATHER THAN
// AT ONE CHOKE POINT.** The sites sit at successive points of one compile, and a module GAINS functions
// between them, so the set of duplicates each can meet grows monotonically. There is no single moment that
// covers all of them (MEASURED, A1w):
//
//   * `ConformanceCheck.checkConformance` — Maxon tier, before `installManagedGlobalInit` and before the
//     pipeline. **It can see NONE.** Its only available cause is two parsed declarations, which is E3006 at
//     merge, and it refuses to read a malformed module (`projectHasErrors`) four lines before it asks for an
//     index; no synthesizer has appended to the module yet. It is the one site that is not in this class —
//     and it builds its index HERE anyway, because another spelling of the same loop costs more than the
//     one integer compare does.
//   * `StdlibSource.reachableMaxonFunctionNames` — Maxon tier, and since S2h it too runs BEFORE
//     `installManagedGlobalInit`, so **it can see NONE** for the reason above. ⚠ It used to be the earliest
//     site that could meet a `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` declaration of `__module_init` /
//     `__maxon_global_cleanup` against the compiler's own, and moving it did not lose that refusal: the next
//     site sees the identical pair, and `DeadFunctionElimination.markCompilerOwnedRoot` catches the case with
//     no pair to count. **That is exactly why this list must be read before an index site is moved** — what
//     a site can see is a property of its POSITION, and the position is now `Compiler.compileToCodeResult`'s
//     ordering decision rather than this walk's.
//   * `IR/PassPipeline.run` — Maxon tier, ONCE for the whole pipeline, over the array `resolveTypes`,
//     `semanticCheck` and `lowerMaxonToStd` all walk (A2l), and the FIRST site downstream of
//     `installManagedGlobalInit`. It can see a declaration of `__module_init` / `__maxon_global_cleanup`
//     against the compiler's own. It replaced a build inside
//     `SemanticCheck.buildParamMutationSummary`, which sat AFTER two other name-keyed maps that pass builds —
//     so those two were right by ORDERING and could not say so; they now take this index as an argument.
//   * `IR/Std/DeadFunctionElimination` — Std tier, after every `install*Runtime`, and TWICE: once over the
//     array it prunes (the walk resolves its call-graph edges through it) and once over the array it leaves
//     behind, which is the one every backend then resolves a callee through. It can see the above plus a
//     declaration of any symbol a runtime installs (`__print_string`, `__str_eq`, `__mm_*`, a `__destruct_<T>`
//     cascade …), which is the pair A1t was written for and the only one no earlier site can reach.
//
// ⚠ **AND ONE CLASS OF COLLISION IS STILL BELOW ALL OF THEM.** The entry stub, the hand-assembled runtime
// chunks and `__symtable` are laid into `.text` under names that are in NO module, so no index here can see
// them; that collision is noticed where the layout is decided (`BackendDispatch.recordChunkLabel`), under
// the same E4015.
//
// ⚠ **THE INDEX DESCRIBES THE ARRAY AS IT WAS AT `create`, AND GOES STALE WITH IT.** Exactly one pass
// replaces a function array — `DeadFunctionElimination.sweepUnreachedFunctions`, which assigns a freshly
// built one — and it builds a REPLACEMENT index over the array it just installed, because the backend reads
// one downstream of it; an index resolved against a DIFFERENT array is the failure `ValueOrigin`'s header
// describes. The array travels INSIDE the record because `funcNamed` needs both halves and they must be the
// same two halves; the callers that only want a position read `byName` and index the module they already
// hold, which is that same array.
//
// ⭐⭐ **EVERY NAME-KEYED MAP DERIVED FROM A FUNCTION ARRAY IS BUILT FROM ONE OF THESE, AND THAT IS WHY
// `functions` IS EXPORTED (A2l).** Ten sites used to hand-roll their own `Map with (ByteArray, …)` over a
// module's function array — the wasm function-index map, the two ABI class maps the native backends thread
// through every call site, the throws map, the escape summary, the layout-descriptor set — each with a bare
// last-wins `upsert` and no check, and every one of them resting on this file's guarantee without standing
// behind it. Ordering made most of them right, and ordering is not something the next reader can see.
//
// So the guarantee is now carried by a TYPE rather than by a comment: a builder that walks `index.functions`
// cannot be called without an index existing, and an index cannot exist without `create` having refused a
// duplicate over THAT ARRAY. Exposing the array is what makes the two halves inseparable — a builder handed
// the module instead could walk a different (or later) array than the one that was checked, which is the
// staleness the paragraph above describes.
//
// ⭐ **AND WHERE THE VALUE IS A FIELD READ, THERE IS NO SECOND MAP AT ALL.** A map from name to
// `func.returnType` is a copy of a fact the index can already reach in two O(1) steps (`byName` →
// `functions.get` → the field), so those sites read THROUGH the index and the map is deleted rather than
// blessed. A map whose value is COMPUTED (a float parameter mask, a wasm valtype list) or whose key set is
// FILTERED stays, because recomputing per lookup would trade a stated guarantee for a real cost — but it is
// built from `index.functions`, so its key set is provably the checked one.

// name -> position in the function array. Unique by construction: `FunctionNameIndex.create` refuses a
// duplicate rather than resolving one.
export typealias FuncIndexByName = Map with (ByteArray, FuncIndex)

// The index, or the position of the function whose name collided with an earlier one. A union rather than
// a throw, so `build`'s caller decides whether a duplicate is ITS refusal to raise — see `build`.
export union FunctionNameIndexBuild
	duplicateAt(laterIndex FuncIndex)
	built(index FunctionNameIndex)
end 'FunctionNameIndexBuild'

export type FunctionNameIndex
	// The array the indices index, carried WITH the map for the reason the header's last paragraph gives.
	// EXPORTED so a derived per-callee map is built by walking the very array the duplicate check ran over —
	// see the header's A2l paragraph.
	export let functions as IrFunctionArray
	export let byName as FuncIndexByName

	// ⭐ **THE REFUSING CONSTRUCTOR — the one every compile pass uses.** Building the index IS the
	// duplicate-name refusal (E4015); see the header for which site can see which collision.
	export static function create(functions IrFunctionArray, project Project) returns FunctionNameIndex throws CompileError
		match FunctionNameIndex.build(functions) 'build'
			duplicateAt(laterIndex) then try refuseDuplicateFunctionName(functions, project: project, laterIndex: laterIndex)
			built(index) then return index
		end 'build'
		panic("FunctionNameIndex.create: `refuseDuplicateFunctionName` returned instead of throwing — every arm of it either reports and throws or panics, and this constructor has no answer for a duplicate it was told about and asked to ignore")
	end 'create'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME WALK, WITHOUT THE REFUSAL — for the one reader that must NOT raise it.** It ANSWERS
	// with the duplicate instead of reporting it, so a caller that is not the compile's authority on E4015
	// can decline to act rather than race the site that is.
	//
	// `IR/Std/PromoteStackRecords` is that caller. It resolves callees by NAME to decide which body is a
	// record constructor and which of its parameters escape, so under a duplicate it would reason about a
	// call using the WRONG body's summary — and a wrong answer there is a dangling pointer. But it runs at
	// the Std tier BEFORE `DeadFunctionElimination`, which is the site the header lists as the one that can
	// see a runtime-symbol collision; raising E4015 from here would move that refusal earlier and change
	// which of the two diagnostics a program gets. So it declines to reason and lets the owning site speak.
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS IS NOT A SECOND TIE-BREAK POLICY, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE HEADER'S FIRST PARAGRAPH.**
	// There is still exactly one walk, one collision test and NO resolution of a tie — the two constructors
	// differ only in who is told. A caller that hand-rolled its own `Map with (ByteArray, FuncIndex)` to get
	// a non-throwing build would have been the eleventh such map and the third tie-break, which is what this
	// exists to prevent.
	export static function build(functions IrFunctionArray) returns FunctionNameIndexBuild
		var byName = FuncIndexByName.create()
		for i in 0 upto functions.count() 'eachFunc'
			let func = try functions.get(i) otherwise panic("FunctionNameIndex.build: functions.get OOB at {i}, inside a loop bounded by its own count")
			byName.upsert(func.name, value: i)
			// The height IS `i + 1` after every insert unless one insert collapsed two names into one entry
			// — one integer compare per function, no second probe. See the header.
			if byName.count() != i + 1 'duplicateName'
				return FunctionNameIndexBuild.duplicateAt(i)
			end 'duplicateName'
		end 'eachFunc'
		return FunctionNameIndexBuild.built(Self{functions: functions, byName: byName})
	end 'build'

	// The function a name denotes. Two lookups rather than a name -> function map, so the ONE checked index
	// above serves a caller that wants the function as well as one that wants its position.
	export function funcNamed(name ByteArray) returns IrFunction throws MapError
		let index = try self.byName.get(name)
		return try self.functions.get(index) otherwise panic("FunctionNameIndex.funcNamed: '{String.from(name)}' maps to position {index}, which is outside the array the index was built over")
	end 'funcNamed'
end 'FunctionNameIndex'

// ⭐⭐ **THREE CAUSES SHARE ONE SYMPTOM, AND PROVENANCE IS WHAT TELLS THEM APART (A1t).** Two of them are
// compiler bugs and must stay a panic; the third is an AUTHOR's mistake and gets a diagnostic:
//
//   * **one side parsed, one side synthesized** — a declaration took a name the compiler owns. E4015,
//     positioned at the declaration. D6 is what opened the door — it let `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` DECLARE
//     reserved `__` names — and what creates the PAIR is whatever makes the compiler install its own copy,
//     which differs by runtime. ⚠ **NOT always a call:** a usage-gated symbol needs one (A1r opened that
//     door, and `__print_string` is installed by `print`/an explicit call), but
//     `__module_init`/`__maxon_global_cleanup` are installed on `globals.count() != 0` alone (`ModuleInit`),
//     so declaring one of THOSE collides with no call anywhere in the program. MEASURED both ways. The check
//     runs before any reachability walk, so an uncalled declaration is diagnosed rather than quietly pruned.
//   * **both synthesized** — an installer ran twice, or one `Project` was compiled twice over the memoized
//     module. A compiler bug.
//   * **both parsed** — two declarations of one name, which `commitFuncSignatures` reports as E3006 at
//     merge. A compiler bug in that check — **but only if nothing has reported it**, which is the one thing
//     this classifier must READ rather than assume; see the arm.
//
// **The discriminator is `sourceFilePath`, and it is DERIVED rather than guessed**: the parser stamps it on
// every function it builds (`parseFunction`, plus `parseClosureExpression` for a lifted closure) and the
// two doors a synthesized function comes through — `IrFunction.createFromStd` and `ModuleInit`'s
// `IrFunction.create` — leave it empty. Nothing between there and here can lose it: the ONE boundary that
// COPIES a function is per-file artifact → merged module (`IrModule.merge` → `relocateFunction` →
// `shallowCopy`, which carries `sourceFilePath`/`nameLine`/`nameColumn` across), and the Maxon→Std transfer
// copies nothing at all — `lowerMaxonToStd` pushes the SAME `IrFunction` object, rewriting only its type
// fields. That is the SAME fact `TargetPrinter.isRuntimeFunction` reads, and it is read the same way for the
// same reason: a NAME test cannot do this job, because the one file that can reach this error is the one
// file whose declarations wear the compiler's own prefix (the A1r review measured a SIGSEGV on exactly that
// confusion).
//
// ⚠ **REFUSE, NEVER REDIRECT.** The A1r review established that steering the CALL to the surviving
// definition trades this refusal for a silent miscompile — `print("x")` would bind to the author's
// declaration instead of the runtime's `__print_string`, which knows the String record's layout. So the
// compile is refused at the duplicate and no binary is produced.
function refuseDuplicateFunctionName(functions IrFunctionArray, project Project, laterIndex FuncIndex) throws CompileError
	let later = try functions.get(laterIndex) otherwise panic("refuseDuplicateFunctionName: functions.get OOB at the index the caller's own loop just read")
	let earlier = earlierFunctionNamed(functions, name: later.name, before: laterIndex)
	let name = String.from(later.name)

	if earlier.sourceFilePath.isEmpty() != later.sourceFilePath.isEmpty() 'oneOfEachProvenance'
		// The parsed side carries the author's position; the synthesized side's is blank by construction,
		// and an unpositioned diagnostic is barely better than the panic it replaces.
		let declared = later if earlier.sourceFilePath.isEmpty() else earlier
		reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.declarationTakesCompilerOwnedName, message: "declaration of '{name}' collides with a symbol the compiler EMITS into this program, and a program cannot hold two functions of one name. The compiler's own definition is the one its emitted code is bound to — the entry stub's calls, the runtime's own call sites, and every call it lowers to that name — so the declaration is the side that must move: rename it", line: declared.nameLine, column: declared.nameColumn, filePath: declared.sourceFilePath)
		throw CompileError.compileError
	end 'oneOfEachProvenance'

	if later.sourceFilePath.isEmpty() 'bothSynthesized'
		panic("refuseDuplicateFunctionName: two SYNTHESIZED functions are named '{name}' — every runtime symbol is installed at exactly one site, so this is an installer that ran twice (or a compile driven twice over one memoized module), and the name index can only keep one of them")
	end 'bothSynthesized'

	// ⭐ **BOTH PARSED, AND WHETHER THAT IS A COMPILER BUG IS NOT A PROPERTY OF THE DUPLICATE — IT IS
	// WHETHER ANYTHING HAS ALREADY SAID SO.** Two declarations of one name is a USER error, and A1t could
	// argue the panic below because its one caller ran at the BACK END, downstream of the pipeline's error
	// gate; most callers now run upstream of that gate, so the argument has to be read off the
	// project instead of inherited from a call site. A program already carrying a diagnostic is already
	// refused: stop, and let the caller's own gate print what was accumulated. Resolving the tie would be
	// the silent half of the very behaviour this file removes.
	if projectHasErrors(project) 'alreadyRefused'
		throw CompileError.compileError
	end 'alreadyRefused'

	// Both positions are printed rather than one cause asserted: two declarations E3006 missed entirely and
	// ONE declaration merged (or lowered) twice both land here, and only the two positions tell them apart.
	// A1t exists because a panic named two causes that were both false for the case in front of it.
	panic("refuseDuplicateFunctionName: two PARSED functions are named '{name}', at {earlier.sourceFilePath.toString()}:{earlier.nameLine} and {later.sourceFilePath.toString()}:{later.nameLine}, and no diagnostic has been reported — two DISTINCT positions mean `commitFuncSignatures`' whole-program E3006 did not see one of them; the SAME position twice means one declaration was merged or lowered into the module twice")
end 'refuseDuplicateFunctionName'

// The function `name` was already bound to before index `before`. It has to be recovered from the function
// ARRAY rather than the map: `upsert`'s last-wins has already replaced the incumbent's index with `before`'s,
// and on the diagnosable cause the incumbent is usually the very side that carries the author's position —
// installers append AFTER the user's source, so the parsed function is the earlier one.
//
// Linear, and that costs nothing: it runs at most once per compile, on a path whose next act is to refuse
// the compile or panic.
function earlierFunctionNamed(functions IrFunctionArray, name ByteArray, before FuncIndex) returns IrFunction
	for i in 0 upto before 'eachEarlier'
		let candidate = try functions.get(i) otherwise panic("earlierFunctionNamed: functions.get OOB at {i}, below an index the caller already read")
		if candidate.name == name 'sameName'
			return candidate
		end 'sameName'
	end 'eachEarlier'

	panic("earlierFunctionNamed: the name index collapsed two entries at function {before} ('{String.from(name)}'), but no function before it carries that name — `upsert` overwrote an entry this scan cannot account for")
end 'earlierFunctionNamed'
